1
00:00:09,931 --> 00:00:13,059
Somewhere,
a small country is going hungry.

2
00:00:14,002 --> 00:00:16,129
- Bull, you look depressed.
- I am.

3
00:00:16,304 --> 00:00:18,135
My word of the month is so obscure...

4
00:00:18,306 --> 00:00:20,797
...I can't find opportunity
to use it in conversation.

5
00:00:20,976 --> 00:00:23,001
- What word is that?
- "Pentimento."

6
00:00:24,546 --> 00:00:27,344
So how about that,
Jets came last week, huh?

7
00:00:27,515 --> 00:00:29,483
Bull, what exactly
does "pentimento" mean?

8
00:00:29,651 --> 00:00:33,678
It's an artistic term referring
to the reappearance of original elements...

9
00:00:33,855 --> 00:00:36,380
...that had been obliterated
by a new outer appearance.

10
00:00:36,558 --> 00:00:41,461
Like that little brown shadow on your head
you affectionately refer to as hair?

11
00:00:43,064 --> 00:00:45,362
Good example.

12
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:46,864
Hey, did you see this?

13
00:00:47,035 --> 00:00:50,402
I remember last month, there was a winning
lottery ticket for $3 million.

14
00:00:50,572 --> 00:00:53,439
Whoever has the ticket
hasn't come forward to claim the money.

15
00:00:53,608 --> 00:00:56,270
Three million. I wonder what I'd get
with that much money?

16
00:00:56,444 --> 00:00:58,002
A between-meal snack?

17
00:00:59,714 --> 00:01:01,648
I wonder why the person
hasn't claimed it.

18
00:01:01,816 --> 00:01:04,114
Talking about the unclaimed
lottery ticket?

19
00:01:04,285 --> 00:01:06,685
Yeah, we were wondering
what could keep someone...

20
00:01:06,855 --> 00:01:10,052
...from claiming $3 million
that is sitting there for the taking.

21
00:01:10,225 --> 00:01:11,852
- Murder.
- Huh?

22
00:01:12,027 --> 00:01:13,619
Now, we talking $3 million.

23
00:01:13,795 --> 00:01:17,891
A lot of people will stick a hatpin
in your ear for a whole lot less.

24
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:19,726
Hi, everybody.

25
00:01:19,901 --> 00:01:21,266
- Hi, Selma.
- Hey, Selma.

26
00:01:21,436 --> 00:01:23,631
I want you to meet Mary Jo Martin.

27
00:01:23,805 --> 00:01:26,399
- Hi.
Hi, Mary Jo.

28
00:01:28,877 --> 00:01:31,937
They're here.

29
00:01:32,113 --> 00:01:37,016
She's from the DA's office, just started,
so they sent her down to observe.

30
00:01:40,021 --> 00:01:41,579
- Who?
- You.

31
00:01:43,024 --> 00:01:45,754
Come on, Mary Jo,
I'll show you where you can freshen up.

32
00:01:45,927 --> 00:01:49,260
- Wait, I know where that is. I'll show her.
- Don't beg.

33
00:01:49,431 --> 00:01:50,762
Sit.

34
00:01:53,068 --> 00:01:54,865
Good boy.

35
00:02:41,349 --> 00:02:43,078
How about you come up to my place...

36
00:02:43,251 --> 00:02:47,153
...and we can browse through
my Ansel Adams prints?

37
00:02:47,322 --> 00:02:48,687
Do you like Ansel Adams?

38
00:02:48,857 --> 00:02:52,054
Second president
of the United States, right?

39
00:02:54,562 --> 00:02:55,859
Yeah. Ha-ha-ha-ha.

40
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:58,828
You know,
a lot of people get those two mixed up.

41
00:02:59,234 --> 00:03:00,565
Ah.

42
00:03:01,136 --> 00:03:03,969
<i>Next case, Your Honor,
People v. Otis Weaver.</i>

43
00:03:04,139 --> 00:03:05,606
Aggravated assault.

44
00:03:05,773 --> 00:03:08,207
- How does the defense plead?
- Not guilty, Your Honor.

45
00:03:08,376 --> 00:03:09,673
Prosecutor?

46
00:03:09,844 --> 00:03:12,108
Slippery Rock, '70,
but I figured after college...

47
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,875
...what the hey, I'm gonna do
a little traveling before I go into law school.

48
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:21,181
I was in this little Swiss village,
and it was a farm house, and we had this...

49
00:03:21,356 --> 00:03:24,917
The next case is ready,
the judge is calling.

50
00:03:25,093 --> 00:03:28,995
And everybody
in the courtroom is watching you.

51
00:03:33,868 --> 00:03:36,962
And that's why the Bill of Rights
is sacred to me.

52
00:03:37,705 --> 00:03:40,572
Sorry, I was talking about the Constitution.
I get emotional...

53
00:03:40,742 --> 00:03:43,040
- Can it, Dan.
- Canning it, sir.

54
00:03:43,511 --> 00:03:46,571
All right, the defense
has entered a plea of not guilty.

55
00:03:46,748 --> 00:03:47,772
Hi.

56
00:03:49,217 --> 00:03:50,684
Hello.

57
00:03:50,852 --> 00:03:51,944
You are?

58
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:54,714
Oh. My name is Leo Baldassare.

59
00:03:54,889 --> 00:03:56,413
Oh, but you can call me Leo.

60
00:03:56,591 --> 00:03:57,751
Yeah, I'd like that, Leo.

61
00:03:57,926 --> 00:04:00,486
But you see, court is in session,
so if you'd excuse us.

62
00:04:00,662 --> 00:04:02,960
Oh, I don't wanna bother anyone.

63
00:04:03,131 --> 00:04:05,395
It's just that I've been here all week...

64
00:04:06,134 --> 00:04:07,158
...watching you.

65
00:04:07,335 --> 00:04:08,632
We're armed, Leo.

66
00:04:08,803 --> 00:04:10,293
- Dan.
- Please, sir.

67
00:04:10,471 --> 00:04:13,372
It's very urgent that I talk to you.

68
00:04:13,541 --> 00:04:14,565
Alone.

69
00:04:16,211 --> 00:04:18,543
Mr. Prosecutor, Miss Young,
if you'd excuse us.

70
00:04:18,713 --> 00:04:20,943
- Perhaps we better stay with you.
- Come here.

71
00:04:21,115 --> 00:04:24,846
Let me explain
the term "line of fire" to you.

72
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:27,117
What is it, Leo?

73
00:04:27,288 --> 00:04:31,281
Well, you see, I'm the person
with the $3-million lottery ticket.

74
00:04:31,459 --> 00:04:33,654
Of course you are.
This is not really the time...

75
00:04:33,828 --> 00:04:36,626
I have it right here with me,
with the newspaper clipping.

76
00:04:36,798 --> 00:04:38,993
Are you on the level?
Is that the ticket?

77
00:04:39,167 --> 00:04:40,532
In a word, yes.

78
00:04:40,702 --> 00:04:43,466
- In two words, holy...
- Leo.

79
00:04:44,205 --> 00:04:45,399
What's going on?

80
00:04:45,573 --> 00:04:49,236
Well, you see,
I've made a decision concerning the ticket...

81
00:04:49,410 --> 00:04:51,844
...and it includes you.

82
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:53,440
Please?

83
00:04:56,818 --> 00:04:58,080
We'll take a 10-minute recess.

84
00:05:00,255 --> 00:05:02,223
Mac, for now,
not a word of this to anyone.

85
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:03,755
- Right.
- This way, Leo.

86
00:05:09,931 --> 00:05:11,762
What was that about?

87
00:05:13,534 --> 00:05:16,469
Well, if you guys are gonna badger me...

88
00:05:17,839 --> 00:05:21,434
Leo, assuming that is real,
why are you coming to me?

89
00:05:21,609 --> 00:05:23,372
I don't want it.

90
00:05:23,544 --> 00:05:25,341
Money makes people unhappy.

91
00:05:25,513 --> 00:05:27,572
I'm happy with what I have.

92
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:34,386
Am I on TV, Leo?

93
00:05:35,890 --> 00:05:37,517
I'm serious, sir.

94
00:05:37,692 --> 00:05:39,819
Please, I want you to take it.

95
00:05:39,994 --> 00:05:41,894
Well, that's very flattering, Leo.

96
00:05:42,063 --> 00:05:44,827
But, hey, I can't take your money.

97
00:05:44,999 --> 00:05:47,229
Oh, I didn't mean for you to keep it.

98
00:05:47,802 --> 00:05:49,394
- Oh.
- Uh...

99
00:05:50,004 --> 00:05:51,801
I want you to give it away.

100
00:05:52,507 --> 00:05:54,099
- Give it away?
- Yes.

101
00:05:54,275 --> 00:05:57,574
To one of those people
that come up before you.

102
00:05:57,745 --> 00:05:59,940
I've been here all week watching...

103
00:06:00,114 --> 00:06:04,210
...and I've decided that you're just about
the wisest man I've ever seen.

104
00:06:04,385 --> 00:06:05,477
Well, thank you, Leo.

105
00:06:05,653 --> 00:06:08,213
Of course,
I don't get out very much, but...

106
00:06:09,257 --> 00:06:10,747
Okay, Leo, I'll think about it.

107
00:06:10,925 --> 00:06:14,884
But for the time being, don't tell anyone
you've got this much money to give away.

108
00:06:15,063 --> 00:06:17,156
There are some unscrupulous
types out there...

109
00:06:17,332 --> 00:06:19,095
...that might come looking for it.

110
00:06:19,734 --> 00:06:22,828
Okay,
where's the geek with the 3 mil?

111
00:06:23,971 --> 00:06:26,838
Mac, didn't I ask you
not to tell anyone about this?

112
00:06:27,008 --> 00:06:28,066
Yeah.

113
00:06:29,744 --> 00:06:31,575
Just checking.

114
00:06:31,746 --> 00:06:33,236
Do you really have the winning ticket?

115
00:06:33,414 --> 00:06:35,974
Mm-hm. But not for long.

116
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:41,713
I came down here looking for someone
who still cares about human decency.

117
00:06:41,889 --> 00:06:43,447
He quit.

118
00:06:44,092 --> 00:06:46,993
Actually, Leo asked me
to give this away for him.

119
00:06:47,428 --> 00:06:48,827
Give?

120
00:06:48,996 --> 00:06:50,258
May I?

121
00:06:50,431 --> 00:06:52,831
There have been counterfeit tickets
going around.

122
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,195
Let's check it with these.

123
00:06:55,370 --> 00:06:56,769
Looks real to me.

124
00:06:56,938 --> 00:07:00,305
To the untrained eye. Hmm.

125
00:07:00,475 --> 00:07:02,340
Dan.

126
00:07:03,177 --> 00:07:05,145
Oops. Heh.

127
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,513
Where is my head?

128
00:07:07,682 --> 00:07:09,843
Leo, why are you doing this?

129
00:07:10,017 --> 00:07:12,042
Because I'm already happy.

130
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:13,744
Don't give us that.

131
00:07:13,921 --> 00:07:14,945
Apparently, it's true.

132
00:07:15,123 --> 00:07:17,921
He just wants to give the money
to someone who needs it more.

133
00:07:18,092 --> 00:07:20,822
What the hell is this world coming to?

134
00:07:20,995 --> 00:07:22,223
We're all set, Your Honor.

135
00:07:22,397 --> 00:07:25,093
Let's get back to work, everybody.

136
00:07:25,767 --> 00:07:28,235
- Hi.
- Hi.

137
00:07:29,904 --> 00:07:32,372
He just gave the judge
$3 million to give away.

138
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:33,564
What a shame.

139
00:07:33,741 --> 00:07:37,768
With that kind of money,
he could've had himself fixed.

140
00:07:39,714 --> 00:07:41,238
Give it to me, Harry.

141
00:07:41,416 --> 00:07:44,943
Dan, I think Leo is looking for someone
a little more needy than you.

142
00:07:45,119 --> 00:07:47,383
Yeah, yeah,
what are they gonna use it for, food?

143
00:07:48,556 --> 00:07:49,580
Everybody wants it.

144
00:07:49,757 --> 00:07:52,248
- I am honest enough to admit it.
- I don't want it.

145
00:07:52,894 --> 00:07:56,352
- The old reverse psychology ploy, huh, Mac?
- I don't want it.

146
00:07:56,764 --> 00:07:58,527
- You're serious, aren't you?
- Yes, sir.

147
00:07:58,699 --> 00:08:02,066
See, my needs aren't that great,
and the combination of my income here...

148
00:08:02,236 --> 00:08:06,696
...and my Army pension lets me maintain
a comfortable and rewarding lifestyle.

149
00:08:06,874 --> 00:08:08,774
- That's sweet, Mac.
- Uh-huh.

150
00:08:08,943 --> 00:08:12,310
Plus my grandfather owns the largest
import business on the East Coast...

151
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:14,971
...and I am his sole heir.

152
00:08:15,149 --> 00:08:19,017
Someday, I'm gonna be swimming in it.

153
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:22,987
Perhaps you can get out of the pool
long enough to do me a favor, Mac.

154
00:08:23,157 --> 00:08:25,921
Call the lottery commission,
have them send somebody down.

155
00:08:26,093 --> 00:08:27,321
Got it.

156
00:08:28,296 --> 00:08:29,763
- Uh, Mac.
- Yeah?

157
00:08:29,931 --> 00:08:31,421
- I've got this extra kidney.
- Oh.

158
00:08:31,599 --> 00:08:34,295
If you should ever need just a little...

159
00:08:34,469 --> 00:08:35,629
What's going on?

160
00:08:35,803 --> 00:08:41,366
Well, Harry is looking for an honest man,
like Diogenes.

161
00:08:41,776 --> 00:08:46,475
Played for the Yankees,
was married to Marilyn Monroe, right?

162
00:08:46,647 --> 00:08:50,811
You know, we almost conversed that time,
didn't we?

163
00:08:52,086 --> 00:08:53,815
Listen, Harry, just a word of advice.

164
00:08:53,988 --> 00:08:56,183
Don't take on the responsibility
of that ticket.

165
00:08:56,357 --> 00:08:58,348
- I didn't say for sure I would.
- I know you.

166
00:08:58,526 --> 00:09:01,017
Some poor schlep asked a favor
and you can't resist.

167
00:09:01,195 --> 00:09:03,220
He did seem bewildered
by the whole thing.

168
00:09:03,397 --> 00:09:05,297
- You're gonna do it.
- I didn't say that.

169
00:09:05,466 --> 00:09:08,162
- Your mind's made up, it's over.
- Billie.

170
00:09:08,336 --> 00:09:11,737
Just remember, Harry,
nice guys finish last.

171
00:09:26,087 --> 00:09:27,111
Knock, knock.

172
00:09:30,157 --> 00:09:32,523
Well, Judge Stone. What a surprise.

173
00:09:32,693 --> 00:09:35,218
You caught me at a bad time, Craven.
I'm here.

174
00:09:35,396 --> 00:09:36,488
That's funny. Very good.

175
00:09:36,664 --> 00:09:39,599
Surprised that scandal sheet you write for
is still in business.

176
00:09:39,767 --> 00:09:42,133
Are you kidding?
Misery is a moneymaker.

177
00:09:44,505 --> 00:09:45,995
Did you read my story last week?

178
00:09:46,173 --> 00:09:50,166
Family in Hackensack
barbecues brother. Ha-ha-ha.

179
00:09:50,344 --> 00:09:51,743
I don't read the food section.

180
00:09:51,913 --> 00:09:53,710
Come on, Your Honor, I need a story.

181
00:09:53,881 --> 00:09:56,281
Little sex, violence,
that's all I'm looking for.

182
00:09:56,450 --> 00:09:59,681
Bad timing, Craven, nothing bloodcurdling
on the docket tonight.

183
00:09:59,854 --> 00:10:01,082
Oh, what a world.

184
00:10:01,255 --> 00:10:04,190
I mean, people have knives,
why don't they use them?

185
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,650
All rise.

186
00:10:07,962 --> 00:10:08,986
Whoops.

187
00:10:09,931 --> 00:10:11,762
I think this is for you.

188
00:10:11,933 --> 00:10:14,367
- That'd be my guess.
- Yeah.

189
00:10:16,003 --> 00:10:18,164
You may be seated.

190
00:10:22,476 --> 00:10:23,500
Are you okay, sir?

191
00:10:23,678 --> 00:10:26,977
I'm nervous about this lottery ticket.
What if this thing is real?

192
00:10:27,148 --> 00:10:29,412
Am I qualified to choose
who should get the money?

193
00:10:29,584 --> 00:10:32,075
And more importantly,
do I have that right to decide?

194
00:10:32,887 --> 00:10:34,411
No.

195
00:10:36,324 --> 00:10:38,884
Take some time, think it over.

196
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:42,188
<i>Okay, Your Honor, first up,
Runyon v. Miller.</i>

197
00:10:42,363 --> 00:10:43,990
Tragic stuff.

198
00:10:44,165 --> 00:10:45,689
We may have a winner.

199
00:10:46,067 --> 00:10:47,762
What we have is a simple assault.

200
00:10:47,935 --> 00:10:51,336
Mr. Miller was walking down the street,
he saw a $5 bill on the sidewalk.

201
00:10:51,505 --> 00:10:52,767
He bent over to pick it up.

202
00:10:52,940 --> 00:10:55,135
Mr. Runyon ran up
and kicked him in the stomach.

203
00:10:55,610 --> 00:10:57,339
- Mr. Runyon?
- Your Honor...

204
00:10:57,511 --> 00:11:00,480
...I was just fired
from my job of 20 years.

205
00:11:00,648 --> 00:11:04,448
When they repossessed my car, I borrowed
my son's bike to ride to the bank...

206
00:11:04,619 --> 00:11:07,884
...to withdraw my entire lifesavings
which I needed to pay the rent.

207
00:11:08,322 --> 00:11:12,452
But on the way back, a car hit me,
sending my money flying all over the street...

208
00:11:12,627 --> 00:11:15,960
...where passer-by started scooping it up
for themselves.

209
00:11:19,033 --> 00:11:20,125
Quite a story, huh?

210
00:11:20,301 --> 00:11:24,761
I laughed, I cried, it became a part of me.

211
00:11:26,607 --> 00:11:29,371
Your Honor, what we're dealing with
is an assault charge...

212
00:11:29,543 --> 00:11:31,170
...not an alleged hit-and-run.

213
00:11:31,345 --> 00:11:32,607
I have a pin in my hip too.

214
00:11:32,780 --> 00:11:34,975
Oh, stop whining.

215
00:11:36,651 --> 00:11:40,451
Mr. Runyon, you say
you don't have a penny to your name?

216
00:11:40,621 --> 00:11:42,555
Nope.

217
00:11:43,457 --> 00:11:47,518
And I won't till the insurance company
sends me the half million.

218
00:11:49,130 --> 00:11:50,461
The prosecution rests.

219
00:11:50,631 --> 00:11:52,599
Comfortably, Your Honor.

220
00:11:53,668 --> 00:11:55,829
Thank you, Mr. Runyon.
Two days suspended.

221
00:11:58,639 --> 00:12:00,937
Oh, excuse me.
I'm looking for Judge Stone.

222
00:12:01,108 --> 00:12:03,599
I'm from the lottery,
I'm here to verify the ticket.

223
00:12:03,778 --> 00:12:05,871
- The ticket? You mean...
- The lottery ticket.

224
00:12:06,047 --> 00:12:09,574
Lottery ticket. Judge Stone was
talking about that. The one that he...

225
00:12:09,750 --> 00:12:12,947
The one he's supposed to give away.
It's unclaimed, worth $3 million.

226
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:14,849
Three million dollars?
Yeah, that's the one.

227
00:12:17,091 --> 00:12:19,025
Next case, Your Honor, lvan Brewster.

228
00:12:19,894 --> 00:12:21,828
Now, he's accused of... Now get this.

229
00:12:21,996 --> 00:12:26,626
- Beating up a mime
and stealing his hat full of change.

230
00:12:26,801 --> 00:12:28,234
Why did you do that?

231
00:12:29,270 --> 00:12:31,135
Kicks.

232
00:12:32,773 --> 00:12:35,173
I wish they were all like you.

233
00:12:35,342 --> 00:12:38,641
Excuse me, Your Honor,
I think you might wanna take a recess.

234
00:12:38,813 --> 00:12:40,781
Sit down before I find you in contempt.

235
00:12:40,948 --> 00:12:43,007
This man is here
from the lottery commission.

236
00:12:43,184 --> 00:12:47,883
Wants to verify the $3-million ticket
you were asked to give away.

237
00:12:48,422 --> 00:12:49,980
Recess.

238
00:12:51,625 --> 00:12:53,286
Your Honor, may I see the ticket, please?

239
00:12:53,461 --> 00:12:54,860
Ooh. Come on, Harry.

240
00:12:55,029 --> 00:12:57,088
Dan, be patient.
This could take a long time.

241
00:12:57,264 --> 00:13:01,030
This man has to examine the ticket,
compare to a master, maybe have it tested.

242
00:13:01,202 --> 00:13:02,999
This baby's good.

243
00:13:03,637 --> 00:13:05,605
And I considered hanging out
at the morgue today.

244
00:13:06,974 --> 00:13:08,066
- Craven.
- What?

245
00:13:08,242 --> 00:13:10,142
For once, can't you do the decent thing?

246
00:13:10,311 --> 00:13:11,403
Be a human being?

247
00:13:11,579 --> 00:13:14,742
Keep this under your hat
and stop my court from becoming a circus?

248
00:13:16,117 --> 00:13:18,585
Naaah.

249
00:13:29,830 --> 00:13:32,025
Hi, Dan.

250
00:13:34,001 --> 00:13:37,095
I just wanted to tell you
I had fun last night.

251
00:13:37,872 --> 00:13:41,740
- Great.
- Oh, and you have great taste in music.

252
00:13:41,909 --> 00:13:45,174
I just loved listening
to that Willie Nelson overture.

253
00:13:47,181 --> 00:13:51,049
No, Mary Jo,
that was the "Willie Tell Overture."

254
00:13:51,552 --> 00:13:53,577
I'm sorry.

255
00:13:53,754 --> 00:13:56,723
Anyway, I'm looking forward
to the party tonight...

256
00:13:56,891 --> 00:14:00,554
...and meeting all your friends.

257
00:14:01,028 --> 00:14:02,655
Bye.

258
00:14:08,068 --> 00:14:11,868
She makes Squiggy
look like Buckminster Fuller.

259
00:14:12,606 --> 00:14:15,769
I had a husband that dumb too.

260
00:14:16,510 --> 00:14:20,378
They fool you by walking upright,
don't they?

261
00:14:27,454 --> 00:14:28,921
How many are in there?

262
00:14:30,424 --> 00:14:32,585
Remember Woodstock?

263
00:14:33,494 --> 00:14:36,224
Courtesy of our favorite yellow journalist.

264
00:14:36,397 --> 00:14:39,332
"Man chucks bucks, judge gets stuck."

265
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:40,990
Kind of catchy.

266
00:14:43,437 --> 00:14:45,667
Whew. Lot of crazies out there.

267
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:49,105
I better get that docket
before one of them eats it.

268
00:14:50,044 --> 00:14:51,068
Hi, judge.

269
00:14:51,245 --> 00:14:52,735
It's quite a turnout, isn't it?

270
00:14:52,913 --> 00:14:55,347
The power of the press.
Kind of gives me goose bumps.

271
00:14:55,516 --> 00:14:58,781
Bull, explain to this man
the penalty for harassing a judge.

272
00:14:59,286 --> 00:15:03,086
Section 4C of the penal code,
wherein the bailiff is encouraged...

273
00:15:03,257 --> 00:15:08,490
...to pull the assailant's ears
behind his head and tie them in a knot.

274
00:15:09,830 --> 00:15:11,957
That's not really a law, is it?

275
00:15:12,433 --> 00:15:15,630
Gotcha. It's probably a regional thing
that I, you know...

276
00:15:23,043 --> 00:15:25,011
Yeah?

277
00:15:25,412 --> 00:15:28,609
Have you seen that courtroom?
We are talking Bronx Zoo out there.

278
00:15:28,782 --> 00:15:31,876
- Go ahead and say it.
- A fool and his money are soon parted.

279
00:15:32,052 --> 00:15:34,850
- "I told you so" would have sufficed.
- Oh, Harry.

280
00:15:35,022 --> 00:15:36,990
There are over 3000
telegrams downstairs...

281
00:15:37,157 --> 00:15:39,648
...from people asking me for money.
- I told you so.

282
00:15:42,062 --> 00:15:43,791
Judge's chambers.

283
00:15:43,964 --> 00:15:46,228
Oh, yes, sure.
Hold on just a moment.

284
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:48,061
It's your father.

285
00:15:50,404 --> 00:15:51,598
Hello?

286
00:15:51,772 --> 00:15:54,104
Dad, yeah. How you doing?

287
00:15:54,275 --> 00:15:55,833
Oh, you sound great, yeah.

288
00:15:56,010 --> 00:15:59,571
Twenty-two years of death
hasn't changed your voice a bit.

289
00:16:00,848 --> 00:16:04,477
- Have you seen that courtroom out there?
- Yeah, I've heard, thank you.

290
00:16:04,652 --> 00:16:10,454
Listen, Harry, in spite of what I've said,
if a decision has to be made on this ticket...

291
00:16:10,624 --> 00:16:12,353
...couldn't be in more capable hands.

292
00:16:12,526 --> 00:16:13,720
Thank you, Billie.

293
00:16:16,931 --> 00:16:20,128
First time you suck up
is always the hardest.

294
00:16:38,152 --> 00:16:40,143
All rise.

295
00:17:04,745 --> 00:17:08,237
You may be seated or fall,
whatever's easier.

296
00:17:09,616 --> 00:17:12,608
First case, Your Honor, Gertrude Stuckey.

297
00:17:12,786 --> 00:17:14,447
Attempted burglary.

298
00:17:15,255 --> 00:17:17,314
She took a case of cat food.

299
00:17:17,691 --> 00:17:19,625
- I don't have a cat.
- Oh?

300
00:17:19,827 --> 00:17:22,057
It was for me
and my husband, Dexter, to eat.

301
00:17:22,229 --> 00:17:23,423
We're very poor.

302
00:17:23,897 --> 00:17:26,127
Well, why didn't you
just steal regular food?

303
00:17:26,734 --> 00:17:28,895
Because cat food is more...

304
00:17:29,603 --> 00:17:31,503
- Pathetic?
- Right.

305
00:17:31,672 --> 00:17:34,641
Wrong. Fifty-dollar fine.

306
00:17:37,845 --> 00:17:41,679
Yeah?
See if you clowns can do any better. Ha!

307
00:17:43,817 --> 00:17:46,377
Next case, Your Honor, Jerome Chappel.

308
00:17:46,553 --> 00:17:50,922
Seems he smashed
up a hospital cafeteria last night.

309
00:17:55,129 --> 00:17:58,098
Your Honor,
due to the unusual nature of the case...

310
00:17:58,265 --> 00:18:01,701
...my client pleads guilty,
but requests a suspended sentence.

311
00:18:01,869 --> 00:18:03,837
I see. How are you, Mr. Chappel?

312
00:18:04,004 --> 00:18:06,632
I have six months to live.

313
00:18:06,807 --> 00:18:08,138
How are you?

314
00:18:08,575 --> 00:18:09,599
Can't complain.

315
00:18:09,777 --> 00:18:13,269
Your Honor, the hospital has no record
of this man even being a patient.

316
00:18:13,447 --> 00:18:16,007
That's a big place.
They can't keep track of everything.

317
00:18:16,183 --> 00:18:17,309
Mr. Chappel...

318
00:18:17,484 --> 00:18:19,509
Look, Your Honor, l...

319
00:18:20,821 --> 00:18:22,755
Your honor, I've always had problems.

320
00:18:22,923 --> 00:18:26,256
When I was growing up, I had a mother
who bought me rabid dogs for pets.

321
00:18:26,427 --> 00:18:28,725
At least you had real pets.

322
00:18:28,896 --> 00:18:31,262
My father gave me rocks
and told me to name them.

323
00:18:31,432 --> 00:18:33,195
Yeah? Well, at least you had a father.

324
00:18:33,367 --> 00:18:35,494
My father left me naked on a doorstep.

325
00:18:35,669 --> 00:18:36,829
At least you had a doorstep.

326
00:18:37,004 --> 00:18:38,938
After my house burned down,
there was nobody.

327
00:18:40,541 --> 00:18:41,769
Oh, people.

328
00:18:43,077 --> 00:18:45,068
People!

329
00:18:50,417 --> 00:18:53,648
In my short time as a judge,
I've seen some pretty amazing things.

330
00:18:53,821 --> 00:18:56,221
But this, I think, tops them all.

331
00:18:59,026 --> 00:19:02,325
Last night,
I witnessed the most considerate...

332
00:19:02,496 --> 00:19:06,728
...selfless, kind act
I think that I have ever seen.

333
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:08,891
Thank you, Your Honor.

334
00:19:11,171 --> 00:19:15,540
And here before me
sits the result of that kindness.

335
00:19:15,709 --> 00:19:20,339
The most selfish, indulgent...

336
00:19:22,382 --> 00:19:27,081
...transparent display
I have ever witnessed.

337
00:19:27,855 --> 00:19:29,482
Leo.

338
00:19:30,224 --> 00:19:34,217
Come take your $3 million back
and spend it with a clear conscience...

339
00:19:34,394 --> 00:19:38,353
...knowing that you
are the most deserving person here.

340
00:19:39,433 --> 00:19:41,492
Your Honor...

341
00:19:41,668 --> 00:19:44,637
...are you sure this is the right thing?

342
00:19:44,805 --> 00:19:47,638
It's the only thing, Leo. Believe me.

343
00:19:47,808 --> 00:19:50,777
A person with your capacity for giving
is very special.

344
00:19:51,445 --> 00:19:53,709
Perhaps it's time
you started giving to yourself.

345
00:19:54,648 --> 00:19:56,912
Besides, you charged me
with the responsibility...

346
00:19:57,084 --> 00:19:59,917
...of finding the most worthy person.

347
00:20:00,654 --> 00:20:02,281
Well, I did.

348
00:20:05,459 --> 00:20:07,086
The money's gone.

349
00:20:12,566 --> 00:20:15,626
Well, I just loved your friend,
Richard, the urologist.

350
00:20:16,036 --> 00:20:17,765
Oh, it must be exciting work.

351
00:20:17,938 --> 00:20:23,433
Going out on those expeditions,
digging around for fossils.

352
00:20:24,645 --> 00:20:27,910
Listen, Mary Jo, you're a very...
Whew.

353
00:20:28,081 --> 00:20:33,951
- Healthy girl and all,
but I'm afraid even I need more than that.

354
00:20:34,755 --> 00:20:35,915
Like what?

355
00:20:37,124 --> 00:20:38,716
Language.

356
00:20:40,394 --> 00:20:42,521
Look, you're here to observe
and that's fine...

357
00:20:42,696 --> 00:20:46,188
...but I'm afraid
I can't see you socially anymore.

358
00:20:47,401 --> 00:20:50,165
Well, if that's the way you feel.

359
00:20:50,337 --> 00:20:51,998
It is.

360
00:20:53,540 --> 00:20:56,236
Well, I've got some work to do.

361
00:20:56,843 --> 00:20:57,969
Bye.

362
00:21:00,647 --> 00:21:02,308
Goodbye.

363
00:21:04,952 --> 00:21:06,010
Well?

364
00:21:06,186 --> 00:21:07,881
Worked like a charm, Selma.

365
00:21:08,055 --> 00:21:12,515
I don't think that slug will be bothering me
again the rest of the time I'm here.

366
00:21:12,693 --> 00:21:14,320
Thanks for tipping me off.

367
00:21:14,494 --> 00:21:17,554
Hey, us foxes have gotta stick together,
don't we?

368
00:21:18,932 --> 00:21:20,524
Pentimento.

369
00:21:20,701 --> 00:21:22,601
I love that word.

370
00:21:22,769 --> 00:21:26,569
You reminded me of it
when your surface rubbed off.

371
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:28,799
The most numerous
examples of pentimento...

372
00:21:28,976 --> 00:21:32,070
...are found among the Dutch works
of the early 17th century.

373
00:21:32,246 --> 00:21:33,838
I know.

374
00:21:35,282 --> 00:21:37,648
Shall we discuss it over some cognac?

375
00:21:37,818 --> 00:21:41,584
Say, at my place after work?

376
00:21:41,755 --> 00:21:44,280
Good God, yes.

377
00:21:48,629 --> 00:21:51,393
Heh-heh.
Those two lunkheads deserve each other.

378
00:21:51,565 --> 00:21:53,089
Yeah.

379
00:21:54,201 --> 00:21:58,365
You know, I still can't believe that I held
$3 million in my hand.

380
00:21:58,538 --> 00:21:59,562
And gave it away.

381
00:21:59,740 --> 00:22:02,231
Yeah, I'm gonna see a doctor about that.

382
00:22:02,409 --> 00:22:05,037
Come on, we're happy the way we are.

383
00:22:05,746 --> 00:22:07,145
- Aren't we?
- Sure.

384
00:22:07,314 --> 00:22:09,680
I make it big, I spend it big.

385
00:22:09,850 --> 00:22:11,943
I'm in for 2 cents.

386
00:22:12,486 --> 00:22:14,579
And I'll see it.

387
00:22:14,755 --> 00:22:18,418
And raise you $5000.

388
00:22:19,426 --> 00:22:20,450
Grandpa will cover it.

389
00:22:23,030 --> 00:22:24,861
- Yeah?
- Hi, everyone.

390
00:22:25,032 --> 00:22:26,124
- Oh, Leo.
- Leo.

391
00:22:26,300 --> 00:22:28,734
- How you doing?
- I want you to meet Arnie Prince.

392
00:22:28,902 --> 00:22:31,132
He's got a way
to help me spend my money.

393
00:22:31,305 --> 00:22:33,205
- Oh. I don't wanna hear this.
- Sure you do.

394
00:22:33,373 --> 00:22:36,069
It's a can't-miss proposition.
We're talking show business.

395
00:22:36,243 --> 00:22:38,734
Leo's putting all his money
into my Broadway musical.

396
00:22:38,912 --> 00:22:43,508
It's what I've always wanted to do,
bring lots of smiles to lots of people.

397
00:22:43,684 --> 00:22:46,118
- Well, that's terrific, Leo.
- What's the play called?

398
00:22:46,620 --> 00:22:49,987
<i>Mussolini: The Man and His Music.</i>

399
00:22:51,525 --> 00:22:54,289
Well, Leo,
we wish you all the success in the world.

400
00:22:54,461 --> 00:22:55,951
Thanks. It's money in the bank.

401
00:22:56,129 --> 00:23:01,396
The only problem I foresee
is finding the right guy for the lead role.

402
00:23:01,902 --> 00:23:02,926
Hello.

403
00:23:06,740 --> 00:23:08,207
Tell me you can sing.

